import PatchLayout from '../../../layouts/PatchLayout.astro';
import PatchSources from '../../../components/PatchSources.astro';
---
<PatchLayout step={4} title="Beyond Patch" description="From Patch to the models used in research: kinds of models and what they are for, how a model earns trust, and LPJmL.">
  <h1 style="margin-top: 1.6rem">From Patch to the models used in research</h1>
  <p class="lead" style="margin-top: 0.5rem">Patch shows the principle. Its processes are real and its equations come from the scientific literature, but it has two kinds of tree, two soil layers and a single block of land, and it reacts more sharply than real vegetation does.</p>
  <p style="margin-top: 0.6rem; max-width: 44rem">The models used in research work on the same principle at a very different scale: tens of thousands of grid cells, many kinds of plant, fire, rivers and nutrients, and years of testing against measurements.</p>

  <section class="sec">
    <h2>Different models for different purposes</h2>
    <div class="tablewrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Kind of model</th><th>What it represents</th><th>Used for</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Dynamic global vegetation model (DGVM)</th><td>Vegetation, carbon and water on all land, as climate changes</td><td>Global carbon and water cycles, shifts in vegetation</td><td>LPJmL, LPJ-GUESS, ORCHIDEE, JULES</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Crop model</th><td>One crop in one field, with detailed management</td><td>Yield, sowing dates, irrigation, fertiliser</td><td>DSSAT, APSIM, WOFOST</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Earth system model</th><td>Atmosphere, ocean, ice and land together</td><td>Climate projections</td><td>CESM, UKESM, MPI-ESM</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Data-driven model</th><td>Patterns found in observations</td><td>Mapping, filling gaps, short-term forecasts</td><td>Regression, random forests, neural networks</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top: 0.6rem">Patch teaches the first family, with a simple crop added. Some research models, LPJmL among them, simulate natural vegetation and crops together.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <h2>How a model earns trust</h2>
    <ol class="trust">
      <li><b>1  Input data</b><p>Weather records, soil maps, CO₂ measurements, land-use maps. A model can only be as good as these.</p></li>
      <li><b>2  Calibration</b><p>Some values in the equations are uncertain. They are adjusted until the model matches a set of measurements.</p></li>
      <li><b>3  Evaluation</b><p>The model is then compared with measurements it was not adjusted to: satellite data, flux towers, river discharge, harvest statistics.</p></li>
      <li><b>4  Model intercomparison</b><p>Several models, built by different teams, run the same experiment. Where they agree, confidence is higher (Warszawski and others 2014).</p></li>
    </ol>
    <div class="calib" id="calib">
      <div>
        <h3>Try calibration yourself</h3>
        <p style="margin-top: 0.4rem">One value in Patch’s photosynthesis equation scales a single leaf up to a whole canopy. It is not known exactly. Turn it until the model line runs through the measured points.</p>
        <div class="knob" style="margin-top: 0.8rem"><label for="alpha"><span>The uncertain value</span><output></output></label><input id="alpha" type="range" min="0.3" max="0.8" step="0.01" value="0.5" /></div>
        <p class="say" aria-live="polite" style="margin-top: 0.4rem"></p>
        <p class="note" style="margin-top: 0.6rem">The points are invented for this demonstration. Real ones come from flux towers, which measure the CO₂ moving between vegetation and the air.</p>
      </div>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 400 172" role="img" aria-label="Monthly photosynthesis: a model line and twelve measured points"></svg>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <h2>Questions these models are used to answer</h2>
    <dl class="terms">
      <dt>Past</dt><dd>How much rain did the Sahara need to be green, as it was 6,000 years ago? Three vegetation models were run to find out (Hopcroft and others 2017).</dd>
      <dt>Present</dt><dd>How much of the CO₂ that people emit is taken up by the land each year? The yearly Global Carbon Budget estimates this with a group of dynamic global vegetation models (Friedlingstein and others 2025).</dd>
      <dt>Future</dt><dd>How will harvests change as the world warms? A set of crop models run with many climate projections found that maize yields could fall by about a quarter by the end of the century if emissions stay high (Jägermeyr and others 2021).</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <h2>What Patch leaves out</h2>
    <p>Nutrients, fire, grazing animals, frost, snow, different soils, pests and people’s choices. It knows two kinds of tree and two kinds of grass, has one soil everywhere, and makes up day-to-day weather from monthly averages.</p>
    <p style="margin-top: 0.6rem; max-width: 46rem">It also reacts too sharply. Its crops lose 9% to 12% of their yield for each degree of warming, where research points to about 6% to 7% for maize and wheat (Zhao and others 2017). Its numbers are illustrations, not predictions. Patch is written from scratch from published equations and shares no code with LPJmL or any other model.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" id="agridry">
    <h2>How AGRI-DRY uses a process-based model</h2>
    <p>In <a href="https://agri-dry.org">AGRI-DRY</a>, Mudit Joshi, who made Patch, uses the process-based model LPJmL to follow crops and natural vegetation together through the last 10,000 years. The aim is to understand how climate shaped where early farmers could grow their crops as farming spread into the drylands of Africa.</p>
    <p style="margin-top: 0.6rem; max-width: 46rem">Measurements from that time are scarce. This is where a process-based model helps: it can be run under a reconstructed past climate and asked what could have grown, and why. <a href="https://agri-dry.org/team/dc/mudit-joshi/">His AGRI-DRY profile</a> · <a href="/work">His work</a></p>
    <p style="margin-top: 1.1rem; max-width: 46rem"><strong>LPJmL</strong> (Lund-Potsdam-Jena managed Land) is a dynamic global vegetation, agriculture and water balance model developed at the Potsdam Institute for Climate Impact Research. It simulates natural vegetation, crops and the water cycle for all land on Earth, in grid cells about 50 km across, with fire, rivers, irrigation, frozen ground and nitrogen (Schaphoff and others 2018; von Bloh and others 2018). Its code is open. <a href="https://www.pik-potsdam.de/en/institute/departments/activities/biosphere-water-modelling/lpjml">LPJmL at the Potsdam Institute</a> · <a href="https://github.com/PIK-LPJmL/LPJmL">Model code</a></p>
  </section>

  <PatchSources keys={['bloh', 'friedlingstein', 'hopcroft', 'jagermeyr', 'schaphoff', 'warszawski', 'zhao', 'climate', 'figure']} />
